  "worked_solution": "Let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+x$ and $Q=-{x}^{3}.$ Then, $P+Q={x}^{3}+x-{x}^{3}=x,$ which is a polynomial of degree $1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. For example, suppose that $P={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $P-Q={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}={x}^{2},$ which is a polynomial of degree $2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, suppose $P=1+{x}^{3}$ and $Q={x}^{3}.$ Then, $\\frac{P}{Q}=\\frac{1+{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}+1,$ which is not a polynomial.\n\nIn conclusion, only statements I and II are true.",
